WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.168 --> 00:00:04.753
Will Donald Trump give clemency to Ross Ulbricht who's serving a life sentence without parole

00:00:04.753 --> 00:00:09.206
for founding and operating the black market e-commerce platform called The Silk Road?

00:00:09.206 --> 00:00:13.100
Please Mr. President, consider my son, Ross Ulbricht.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.522
Please give him a second chance. He won't let you down.

00:00:16.522 --> 00:00:19.110
The case for commuting Ulbricht's sentence is simple.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.143
He never directly harmed anyone and his sentence was wildly disproportionate

00:00:23.143 --> 00:00:26.010
to the severity of the crimes for which he was convicted.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.003
Life sentence without parole is shockingly extreme.

00:00:29.003 --> 00:00:33.920
The bluntness by which the judge communicated why she was pronouncing the sentence,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.970
what took everyone really by surprise.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.266
At that fateful hearing in 2015,

00:00:38.266 --> 00:00:41.289
US District Judge Katherine Forrest told the courtroom

00:00:41.289 --> 00:00:45.104
that she had decided to go well beyond the mandatory minimum of 10 years

00:00:45.104 --> 00:00:49.270
because Ulbricht had taken a philosophical stand against drug prohibition.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.420
The Silk Road's creator thought that he was better than the laws of this country.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.350
This is deeply troubling, terribly misguided, and very dangerous.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.680
The Silk Road stated purpose she said,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.378
was to be beyond the law, which is exactly right and why prohibitionists should have instead tried

00:01:05.378 --> 00:01:08.050
to learn something from Ulbricht's creation.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:14.889
America's public enemy number one in the United States is drug abuse.

00:01:14.889 --> 00:01:17.200
In order to fight and defeat this enemy

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.900
it is necessary to wage a new all out offensive.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.324
What Judge Forrest failed to see is that what's actually troubling and misguided

00:01:26.324 --> 00:01:30.880
is the US war on drugs which has cost the public more than a trillion dollars,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.316
fueled decades of crime and violence, and stolen life years away from millions of Americans.

00:01:36.316 --> 00:01:41.020
Ulbricht set out to disrupt the drug war by moving sales to an online marketplace,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.910
impervious to government interference,

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:46.213
where consenting adults could buy and sell anything of their choosing.

00:01:46.213 --> 00:01:52.256
He aimed to abolish the use of coercion and aggression against mankind, according to his old LinkedIn profile,

00:01:52.256 --> 00:01:55.566
and create an economic simulation to give people

00:01:55.566 --> 00:02:01.879
a firsthand experience of what it would be like to live in a world without the systemic use of force.

00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:05.690
His arguments are incredibly relatable.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:10.040
You know, he argues very succinctly, okay.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:15.660
Why is it that people are thrown in jail for buying weed or magic mushrooms?

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.030
When it comes to that, I think I completely agree with Ross

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.550
and his arguments and his ideas.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:28.610
Where I started to have a really hard time was with the idea of selling things like heroin and fentanyl.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:33.880
Vanity Fair's Nick Bilton argues that the Silk Road caused irreparable harm to others,

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:38.370
citing the six people who allegedly died from drugs they had purchased on the Silk Road,

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.120
including a teenager in Australia who had an adverse reaction to a hallucinogen

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.410
and had jumped out of a hotel window.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:49.730
Those deaths were tragic and heartbreaking, and jurors were right to be moved.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.660
But the real culprit is the drug war itself,

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:56.490
because that's what drove the narcotics trade underground in the first place.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:02.400
That's why there are no reputable brands or quality control in heroine, LSD, or MDMA,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.970
meaning users have no choice but to take dealers at their word.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.480
The Silk Road was an, albeit, imperfect attempt

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.190
to correct for the lack of information in drug markets,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.590
which is why, on net, it probably saved lives.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:17.173
A study in the International Journal of Drug Policy

00:03:17.173 --> 00:03:22.850
found that users flocked to the Silk Road out of concern for street drug quality and personal safety.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:28.860
And vendor selection appeared to be based on trust, speed of transaction, stealth modes, and quality of product.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:33.230
Several users on the site's forums regularly answered questions about drug safety

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.780
and what to do in the case of an overdose.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.430
The Silk Road made buying drugs too easy according to a mother

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.880
whose son struggled with addiction before dying of an overdose.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.030
But shutting down Silk Road didn't make drugs less readily available,

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.800
it only pushed sales onto less trustworthy platforms.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.020
If the president does give Ulbricht clemency, much of the credit belongs to his mother, Lyn,

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:00.000
who has spent the last five years tirelessly advocating on his behalf.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.609
Rehabilitation is done.

00:04:01.609 --> 00:04:04.779
This is about punishment and making him an example.

00:04:04.779 --> 00:04:09.071
Ulbricht's critics often point to a series of contract killings that he allegedly ordered

00:04:09.071 --> 00:04:13.426
with the intention of stopping blackmailers who were threatening to bring him down.

00:04:13.426 --> 00:04:15.866
But those allegations remain unproven.

00:04:15.866 --> 00:04:17.916
The alleged murders were never carried out

00:04:17.916 --> 00:04:21.000
and the government never charged him on those counts.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.643
It's extremely irresponsible to fall hard on one side or the other

00:04:27.643 --> 00:04:32.659
of allegations involving murder that have not been charged much less proven.

00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:36.829
Perhaps the Silk Road's most enduring legacy is bootstrapping Bitcoin,

00:04:36.829 --> 00:04:41.043
as New York times Reporter, Nathaniel Popper, explained in 2015.

00:04:41.043 --> 00:04:43.803
Silk Road proved that Bitcoin worked.

00:04:43.803 --> 00:04:48.233
It proved that you could actually send real value across the world.

00:04:48.233 --> 00:04:54.176
And people were willing to try that experiment because they had an incentive to,

00:04:54.176 --> 00:04:58.979
because getting heroin that way was that much better than the alternative.

00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:01.876
Freeing Ross Ulbricht would be the most appropriate way

00:05:01.876 --> 00:05:03.896
to cap off a year in which American voters

00:05:03.896 --> 00:05:07.793
made it loud and clear that they're ready to close the book on the drug war.

00:05:07.793 --> 00:05:16.040
What frustrates me the most is you have everyone out there defending Ross.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:22.493
But I don't see anyone out there defending all these poor people who are in jail

00:05:22.493 --> 00:05:26.383
for selling weed or magic mushrooms, who are African-American and Hispanic.

00:05:26.383 --> 00:05:32.328
If Ulbricht's supporters really cared about the war on drugs or libertarian ideals, Bilton opined,

00:05:32.328 --> 00:05:35.020
they'd be demanding that the nearly half a million people

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.686
currently in US jails for drug offenses should be pardoned too.

00:05:38.686 --> 00:05:45.219
And that's exactly what libertarians have been saying going back to the 1970s.

00:05:45.219 --> 00:05:50.223
Prior to his sentencing, Ross Ulbricht wrote a letter to the judge pleading for mercy.

00:05:50.223 --> 00:05:53.372
"I've had my youth and I know you must take away my middle years,

00:05:53.372 --> 00:05:56.069
but please leave me my old age.

00:05:56.069 --> 00:06:00.513
I certainly won't be the rebellious risk taker I was when I created Silk Road.

00:06:00.513 --> 00:06:02.603
In fact, I'll be an old man."

00:06:02.603 --> 00:06:05.049
Ross Ulbricht turned 36 this year.

00:06:05.049 --> 00:06:09.772
 Let's hope he's still a rebel, and soon enough, once again, a free man.

